name: archives
boss: Clobber
flavor: Unpack me carelessly and I scatter across everything you own.
brief: tar bundles many files into one. It does not compress by itself -- -z
brief: pipes the bundle through gzip, -j through bzip2. That is why you see
brief: czf and xzf so often: create/extract, compress, file.
brief:
brief: Always look before you extract. tar tf lists the contents without
brief: writing anything, and an archive that expands into the current
brief: directory instead of a subdirectory will scatter files everywhere.
brief:
brief: -c here means stdout, not count.
---
setup: you just downloaded @archive@ and don't trust it yet
q: %list% @archive@'s contents with tar, not extracting
a: tar -tf @archive@
out: ^[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+/?$
ref: tar
teach: tar t
teach: tar -f
hint: t lists, and -f names the archive file to read
---
q: %list% @archive@'s sizes and permissions, with tar
a: tar -tvf @archive@
out: ^[-d][-rwx]{9}
ref: tar
teach: tar -v
hint: v adds the verbose per-entry detail, sizes and permissions
---
q: %make% bundle.tar containing @dir@, with tar
a: tar -cf bundle.tar @dir@
state: exists:bundle.tar
ref: tar
teach: tar c
hint: c creates a new archive; f names the file to write it to
---
q: %make% a gzip-compressed bundle.tar.gz containing @dir@, with tar
a: tar -czf bundle.tar.gz @dir@
state: gz:bundle.tar.gz
ref: tar
teach: tar -z
hint: z pipes the archive through gzip as it's written
---
q: %make% a bzip2-compressed bundle.tar.bz2 containing @dir@, with tar
a: tar -cjf bundle.tar.bz2 @dir@
state: bz:bundle.tar.bz2
ref: tar
teach: tar -j
hint: j pipes the archive through bzip2 instead of gzip
---
q: %unpack% archive/export.tar into the current directory, with tar
a: tar -xf archive/export.tar
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar x
hint: x extracts; with no -C it lands in the current directory
---
q: %unpack% archive/site-backup.tar.gz into @dir@, not scattered here, with tar
a: tar -xzf archive/site-backup.tar.gz -C @dir@
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar -C
hint: -C changes into that directory before extracting
---
q: {peek at src/main.c in archive/site-backup.tar.gz with tar, not the rest|read src/main.c out of archive/site-backup.tar.gz to your terminal, with tar|pull src/main.c from archive/site-backup.tar.gz with tar, nothing else}
a: tar -xzOf archive/site-backup.tar.gz src/main.c
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar -O
hint: O sends the extracted file's bytes to stdout instead of writing it to disk
---
q: %make% bundle.tar from @dir@, skipping .log files, with tar
a: tar -cf bundle.tar --exclude '*.log' @dir@
state: exists:bundle.tar
ref: tar
teach: tar --exclude
hint: --exclude takes a pattern and skips anything that matches it
---
q: %unpack% archive/site-backup.tar.gz without its top-level wrapper, with tar
a: tar -xzf archive/site-backup.tar.gz --strip-components 1
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar --strip-components
hint: --strip-components N removes that many leading path segments
---
setup: your last extraction half-finished and left files behind
q: %unpack% archive/site-backup.tar.gz overwriting anything in the way, with tar
a: tar -xzf archive/site-backup.tar.gz --overwrite
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar --overwrite
hint: --overwrite lets extraction replace files already in the way
---
q: %make% bundle.tar from the list of names held in names.txt, with tar
a: tar -cf bundle.tar -T names.txt
state: exists:bundle.tar
ref: tar
teach: tar -T
hint: -T reads the list of names to archive from a file instead of the command line
---
q: {bundle @dir@ into bundle.tar with tar, not descending into it|use tar --no-recursion so @dir@ lands flat inside bundle.tar|archive @dir@ into bundle.tar flat, with tar, skipping inside it}
a: tar -cf bundle.tar --no-recursion @dir@
state: exists:bundle.tar
ref: tar
teach: tar --no-recursion
hint: --no-recursion archives a directory entry itself without descending into it
---
q: %unpack% archive/site-backup.tar.gz, ignoring recorded modes, with tar
a: tar -xzf archive/site-backup.tar.gz --no-same-permissions
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar --no-same-permissions
hint: --no-same-permissions applies your umask instead of the recorded modes
---
q: %unpack% archive/site-backup.tar.gz, skipping owner lookups, with tar
a: tar -xzf archive/site-backup.tar.gz --numeric-owner
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar --numeric-owner
hint: --numeric-owner keeps uid/gid numbers instead of resolving to names
---
q: {compress @txt@ with gzip, replacing it with @txt@.gz|use gzip on @txt@, which becomes @txt@.gz in place|gzip @txt@ so the original is replaced by its .gz}
a: gzip @txt@
state: exists:@txt@.gz
ref: gzip
teach: gzip
hint: gzip replaces the original file with a .gz version by default
---
q: {compress @txt@ with gzip -- you still need the original after|gzip @txt@ without losing @txt@ itself|compress @txt@ with gzip, but keep @txt@ around too}
a: gzip -k @txt@
state: exists:@txt@
ref: gzip
teach: gzip -k
hint: -k keeps the original file alongside the new .gz
---
q: {gzip @txt@ into out.gz, @txt@ itself untouched|with gzip, send @txt@'s compressed bytes to out.gz, unchanged|send @txt@'s gzip output to out.gz, @txt@ left alone}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > out.gz
state: exists:@txt@
ref: gzip
teach: gzip -c
hint: -c writes the compressed bytes to stdout, leaving the file untouched
---
q: {a leftover @txt@.gz exists -- compress @txt@ anyway, with gzip|@txt@.gz already exists -- redo the compression, with gzip|compress @txt@ again though its .gz is already there, with gzip}
a: gzip -f @txt@
state: exists:@txt@.gz
ref: gzip
teach: gzip -f
hint: -f overrides gzip's refusal to clobber an existing .gz
---
q: {gzip @txt@ to z.gz, then read it without unpacking to disk, with zcat|print z.gz's contents, from @txt@, with zcat, no extra write|stream z.gz's contents, built from @txt@, to your terminal, with zcat}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > z.gz && zcat z.gz
out: rotate
ref: zcat
teach: zcat
hint: zcat is gzip -dc -- decompress straight to stdout
---
q: {compress @txt@ into z.gz, then uncompress it back to z, with gunzip|use gunzip to turn z.gz, gzipped from @txt@, back into z|reverse gzip's compression on z.gz, built from @txt@, with gunzip}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > z.gz && gunzip z.gz
state: exists:z
ref: gunzip
teach: gunzip
hint: gunzip is the inverse of gzip, replacing the .gz with the original
---
q: {make z.gz from @txt@, then confirm it isn't corrupt, with gunzip|verify z.gz, gzipped from @txt@, decompresses clean, with gunzip|check z.gz, from @txt@, isn't corrupt, no output, with gunzip}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > z.gz && gunzip -t z.gz
status: 0
state: exists:z.gz
ref: gunzip
teach: gunzip -t
hint: -t verifies the archive's integrity without writing any output
---
q: {gzip @txt@ to z.gz, print its contents, z.gz stays, with gunzip|read z.gz's contents, from @txt@, without deleting it, with gunzip|stream z.gz, gzipped from @txt@, to stdout, kept, with gunzip}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > z.gz && gunzip -c z.gz
out: rotate
ref: gunzip
teach: gunzip -c
hint: -c decompresses to stdout, leaving the .gz file in place
---
q: {compress @txt@ with bzip2 rather than gzip|use bzip2 instead of gzip on @txt@|run bzip2 on @txt@ in place of gzip}
a: bzip2 @txt@
state: exists:@txt@.bz2
ref: bzip2
teach: bzip2
hint: bzip2 works like gzip but usually compresses tighter, at more cpu cost
---
q: {@txt@ needs to be as small as possible -- max bzip2 strength|squeeze @txt@ down as far as bzip2 can, cpu cost be damned|compress @txt@ with bzip2's strongest, slowest setting}
a: bzip2 -9 @txt@
state: exists:@txt@.bz2
ref: bzip2
teach: bzip2 -1..9
hint: -9 is the tightest compression level; -1 is the fastest
---
q: {compress @txt@ with bzip2 into out.bz2, @txt@ untouched|send @txt@'s bytes, compressed with bzip2, to out.bz2, unchanged|bzip2 @txt@ down to out.bz2, keeping the source file as is}
a: bzip2 -c @txt@ > out.bz2
state: exists:@txt@
ref: bzip2
teach: bzip2 -c
hint: -c writes the compressed bytes to stdout instead of replacing the file
---
q: {bzip2 @txt@ to z.bz2, then uncompress z.bz2 back to z, with bunzip2|use bunzip2 to turn z.bz2, from @txt@, back into z|decompress z.bz2 with bunzip2, from @txt@, to its original}
a: bzip2 -c @txt@ > z.bz2 && bunzip2 z.bz2
state: exists:z
ref: bunzip2
teach: bunzip2
hint: bunzip2 is bzip2's inverse, same relationship gunzip has to gzip
---
q: {bzip2 @txt@ to z.bz2, then confirm it isn't corrupt, with bunzip2|verify z.bz2, bzip2'd from @txt@, decompresses clean, with bunzip2|make sure z.bz2, from @txt@, isn't corrupt, with bunzip2}
a: bzip2 -c @txt@ > z.bz2 && bunzip2 -t z.bz2
status: 0
state: exists:z.bz2
ref: bunzip2
teach: bunzip2 -t
hint: -t tests integrity without writing any decompressed output
---
setup: you just downloaded archive.zip
q: {unpack archive.zip into the current directory|extract archive.zip here, no target directory needed|extract every file in archive.zip right here}
a: unzip archive.zip
state: exists:hello.txt
ref: unzip
teach: unzip
hint: unzip with no flags extracts everything into the current directory
---
q: %list% what is inside archive.zip without extracting it, with unzip
a: unzip -l archive.zip
out: hello.txt
cmp: ref
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -l
hint: -l lists contents without extracting anything
---
q: {unpack archive.zip into @dir@ instead of scattering it here, with unzip|keep archive.zip's contents contained -- extract it into @dir@, with unzip|extract archive.zip somewhere specific, @dir@, not here, with unzip}
a: unzip archive.zip -d @dir@
state: exists:@dir@/hello.txt
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -d
hint: -d names the directory to extract into instead of the current one
---
q: {old files in the way -- re-extract archive.zip with unzip, overwrite|redo the unzip extraction of archive.zip, replacing what's there|extract archive.zip again without it asking about existing files, with unzip}
a: unzip -o archive.zip
state: exists:hello.txt
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -o
hint: -o overwrites existing files without prompting for confirmation
---
q: %show% hello.txt from archive.zip to stdout, not a file on disk, with unzip
a: unzip -p archive.zip hello.txt
out: hello from the archive
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -p
hint: -p extracts one named file to stdout instead of writing it
---
q: {you just downloaded archive.zip -- make sure it isn't corrupt, with unzip|verify archive.zip is intact without extracting anything, with unzip|check archive.zip's integrity before you unpack it, with unzip}
a: unzip -t archive.zip
status: 0
cmp: ref
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -t
hint: -t tests the archive's integrity without extracting anything
---
q: %unpack% firmware.tar.xz into @dir@ with tar
a: tar -xJf firmware.tar.xz -C @dir@
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar -J
hint: -J routes the archive through xz, the way -z does for gzip and -j for bzip2
---
q: decompress @word@.xz with xz, keeping the .xz file as well
a: xz -dk @word@.xz
why: fixture-cost
ref: xz
teach: xz -d
teach: xz -k
hint: -d decompresses; -k keeps the .xz instead of replacing it with the output
---
q: decompress @word@.xz to stdout with xz, writing nothing to disk
a: xz -dc @word@.xz
why: fixture-cost
ref: xz
teach: xz -c
hint: -c sends the result to stdout; pair it with -d to decompress there
---
q: check @word@.xz is intact with xz, without unpacking it
a: xz -t @word@.xz
why: fixture-cost
ref: xz
teach: xz -t
hint: -t tests the compressed stream and stays silent unless it is corrupt
---
q: decompress @word@.xz with unxz, the plain decompressor
a: unxz @word@.xz
why: fixture-cost
ref: unxz
teach: unxz
hint: unxz is xz -d under another name -- it only ever decompresses
---
q: {stream @word@.xz to the screen with xzcat|%show% @word@.xz's contents with xzcat, without unpacking it to disk}
a: xzcat @word@.xz
why: fixture-cost
ref: xzcat
teach: xzcat
hint: xzcat is xz -dc -- decompress straight to stdout, as zcat does for gzip
---
q: pack @txt@ into a newc-format archive out.cpio with cpio
a: echo @txt@ | cpio -o -H newc > out.cpio
state: exists:out.cpio
ref: cpio
teach: cpio -o
teach: cpio -H
hint: -o creates from a list of names on stdin; -H newc picks the portable format
---
q: %list% what is inside archive.cpio with cpio, without extracting
a: cpio -t < archive.cpio
out: manifest.txt
ref: cpio
teach: cpio -t
hint: -t lists the members; feed the archive in on stdin
---
q: extract archive.cpio with cpio, creating the directories its members need
a: cpio -id < archive.cpio
state: exists:rescue/manifest.txt
ref: cpio
teach: cpio -i
teach: cpio -d
hint: -i extracts from stdin; -d makes the leading directories as it goes
